  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nCurrent early morning satellite water vapor imagery shows an upper \nlevel wave moving eastward from Washington state towards Montana. \nThis system will continue to track eastward across North Dakota by \nthis evening, thus causing a cold front to push southward into \nnorthwest Kansas later tonight. Scattered showers/storms will \naccompany the front with some activity possibly spreading into parts \nof central Kansas towards daybreak on Tuesday. Better chances will \nremain north of the area. Meanwhile, models show another upper level \nwave moving across the Rockies and ejecting eastward across the \nDakotas/Nebraska late Tuesday night. More numerous showers/storms \nlook to develop in response to modest 700mb warm advection from this \nupper wave over southern Nebraska/northern Kansas for late Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning. A frontal boundary will push further \nsouth across Kansas on Wednesday with better chances of \nshowers/storms possible. Clouds and residual showers/storms \ncould keep daytime highs on Wednesday a little cooler, \nespecially for central Kansas. There is some potential for a few\nstrong to marginally severe storms to materialize on \nWednesday/Wednesday night with increasing wind speeds aloft, \nhowever the residual clouds could become a limiting factor for \ninstability build up. \n\nStorm chances will continue for Thursday/Thursday night and the \nlocation will depend on Wednesday's action which could push the \neffective front further south. If this happens then storm chances \nwould mainly impact southern Kansas into Oklahoma this period. Once \nagain a few strong to marginally severe storms are possible with \nsome increased wind flow aloft. \n\nLonger range models are showing upper level high pressure and \nassociated heat dome migrating eastward back over the central \nplains. This could bring the triple digit daytime highs back to the \narea for next weekend along with dry weather.",
